INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(1,'hep-ph/0011335v3',1,'The inspected sections formulate a renormalizable Standard Model extension with one real gauge-singlet scalar S and an imposed sign symmetry S → −S. The source specifies three additional parameters through the singlet mass term, quartic self-interaction and Higgs interaction −λS²H†H. It chooses an electroweak-breaking vacuum with ⟨S⟩ = 0 to preserve the sign symmetry and protect the proposed dark-matter particle against decay. In that phase it gives m_S² = m_0² + λv_EW² and the associated S²h and S²h² interactions. The source also states vacuum restrictions, but its Higgs-mass convention and coexistence inequality contain apparent internal inconsistencies that remain explicit in this extraction.','Generated summary covers the model definition and vacuum conventions in §2 with introductory context; it does not summarize or validate the relic-abundance, detector or collider calculations. Source images were checked for consequential equation transcription; scientific review remains unchecked.','2026-10-03T21:17:50+00:00','P01-20261003T211139Z-ace5c0c4','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(2,'1306.4710v5',1,'The principal construction is a real scalar singlet S with an imposed Z₂ symmetry and a Higgs portal written as ½λhS S²|H|², giving mS² = μS² + ½λhS v₀² after electroweak symmetry breaking. Equation (1) omits the singlet kinetic and quartic terms, but the prose explicitly acknowledges them; linear and cubic terms in S are instead excluded by the imposed symmetry. The main phenomenological study assumes thermal freeze-out, allows S to supply only part of the dark matter, and uses mass and portal coupling as its plotting parameters. The electroweak-transition application restores explicit dependence on λS S⁴ and permits temporary Z₂ breaking at the critical temperature, although its stated quartic scan ranges conflict internally. A separately discussed complex-singlet generalization has two canonically normalized real components and a differently written portal coefficient, providing a source-specific convention comparison without establishing identity with P01.','Complete for P02''s structural comparison scope. Assertions distinguish defining operators, omitted terms, inferred vacuum properties, study assumptions, and scoped generalizations. No cross-paper agreement or canonical identity is decided.','2026-10-03T22:50:21+00:00','P02-20261003T224404Z-773360fa','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(3,'1106.0034v3',1,'The scoped review descriptions distinguish Type I, in which all quarks and charged leptons couple to Φ2, from Type II, in which up-type quarks couple to Φ2 and down-type quarks and charged leptons to Φ1. These assignments realize natural flavour conservation through different fermion extensions of a Z2 symmetry, despite sharing the same two-doublet gauge representations. The early-chapter treatment assumes a CP-conserving scalar sector and real neutral vacuum expectation values, with symmetry-odd quartics excluded but a real quadratic soft-breaking term allowed. The Yukawa labels therefore specify interaction patterns without fixing scalar masses, mixing angles, or a unique choice between exact and softly broken scalar symmetry; the later symmetry discussion also allows broader CP possibilities outside the early-chapter assumptions.','Summary covers Type-I/II defining interactions, shared scalar assumptions, and symmetry realization only. Numerical collider constraints, benchmark scans, and other constructions are deferred. Source coupling signs require care as detailed in assertions sgn1 and sgn2.','2026-10-04T03:01:41+00:00','P03-20261004T025141Z-eb7ddeca','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(4,'1005.5160v1',1,'The paper defines a minimal left-right theory with gauge group SU(2)L × SU(2)R × U(1)B−L, left- and right-handed quark and lepton doublets, one scalar bidoublet and two scalar triplets. A right-triplet vacuum expectation value breaks the gauge symmetry to the Standard Model, followed by electroweak breaking through the bidoublet and a small induced left-triplet expectation value. Generalized parity makes the quark Yukawa matrices Hermitian; a complex bidoublet vacuum can spoil mass-matrix Hermiticity, although the paper finds closely related left and right quark mixing matrices. Generalized charge conjugation instead makes the Yukawa and quark mass matrices symmetric, giving VR = Ku VL* Kd with equal mixing angles and five additional physical phases. The heavy bidoublet doublet mediates flavor-changing neutral interactions, while the adopted seesaw scenario contains Majorana right-handed neutrinos and lepton-number-violating interactions. The paper attributes substantially different CP constraints to the two discrete implementations and argues that charge conjugation permits a right-handed gauge-boson scale around 2–3 TeV under its phenomenological assumptions.','Complete for the structural seed scope using the supplied text. Numerical statements are retained as conditional claims of this pinned paper, not updated bounds or independent validation. Detailed flavor fits, collider simulations and decay-rate calculations are deferred.','2026-10-04T03:11:11+00:00','P04-20261004T031111Z-5112fe96','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(5,'0910.1785v5',1,'The inspected discussion defines the general NMSSM as the MSSM plus one gauge-singlet chiral superfield, with the Section 2.1 construction restricted to conserved R-parity and CP. It specifies the renormalisable Higgs and matter superpotentials, scalar soft terms and separate MSSM-like gaugino soft masses. The Z3-invariant specification removes the dimensionful Higgs-superpotential terms and their associated soft bilinear and tadpole terms, leaving an accidental common-phase Z3 symmetry and an effective μ parameter μeff = λs. The paper distinguishes this restriction from the field-redefinition convention μ = 0 in the general NMSSM, and gives six Higgs-sector inputs for the Z3 case at fixed MZ plus five additional inputs for the general case. Around the assumed real neutral vacuum, the component content includes three CP-even neutral Higgs states, two physical CP-odd neutral Higgs states and five neutralinos.','This summary concerns the two baseline specifications and their definition conventions; the many constrained, extended and symmetry-violating scenarios elsewhere in the review are deferred.','2026-10-04T03:19:02+00:00','P05-20261004T031136Z-59e19cf5','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(6,'hep-ph/0412089v2',1,'The paper specifies a large-N strong sector with global SU(3)c×SO(5)×U(1)B−L spontaneously broken to SU(3)c×SO(4)×U(1)B−L, yielding a composite Higgs in the SO(5)/SO(4) coset. Elementary Standard Model fields gauge a subgroup and couple to strong-sector operators, explicitly breaking SO(5); top-induced vacuum misalignment can break electroweak symmetry while retaining custodial SO(3). A corresponding weakly coupled theory on an AdS5 interval implements these symmetries through distinct UV and IR boundary groups and identifies the Higgs with physical coset components of A5. Integrating out the bulk at fixed UV values reproduces the 4D form-factor description, with UV sources representing external fields and the bulk representing the strong sector; the microscopic strong dynamics is not identified. The paper also describes additional fermionic and scalar sources of SO(5) breaking that deform the Higgs potential.','The summary concerns the construction and its descriptions. Historical precision constraints, collider forecasts and full numerical scans are not summarized; separate assertions preserve the principal calculability and precision-test qualifications.','2026-10-04T03:22:39+00:00','P06-20261004T031903Z-0bfe3057','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(7,'1404.2640v3',1,'The paper specifies the Georgi–Machacek scalar sector using a complex electroweak doublet with Y = 1, a real triplet with Y = 0, and a complex triplet with Y = 2, organized into a global SU(2)L × SU(2)R bidoublet and bitriplet. Its general gauge- and custodial-invariant tree-level potential includes five quartic couplings and two cubic interactions with coefficients M1 and M2, without imposing the triplet sign symmetry. Equal neutral triplet vevs preserve custodial SU(2), with vφ² + 8vχ² = v², and the physical scalars form a custodial fiveplet, triplet, and two mixed singlets. The aligned stationary point must also satisfy stability requirements and be checked against deeper custodial-breaking vacua. Decoupling is a parameter regime of this same potential: large positive μ3² with M1 and M2 growing at most as μ3 yields heavy, increasingly degenerate additional scalars, vanishing triplet vev and singlet mixing, and SM limits for the light-Higgs couplings.','Complete for the requested scalar construction, symmetry, potential, vacuum, and linked decoupling regime. Detailed phenomenological scans, the full interaction-vertex catalogue, loop integrals, and the separate 2HDM construction are not extracted.','2026-10-04T03:30:26+00:00','P07-20261004T032241Z-0ef112c1','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(8,'1210.6553v2',1,'The paper classifies finite, realizable symmetries of the renormalizable three-Higgs-doublet scalar potential under kinetic-term-preserving Higgs-family and generalized-CP transformations, with gauge-trivial overall phases factored out. Realizability requires the imposed group to be the full symmetry group within the chosen transformation domain. The first detailed extraction targets are the explicitly constructed D6 and D8 Higgs-family potentials, including their generators, coefficient constraints and generic realizability arguments. D6 can remain explicitly CP-violating or acquire D6 × Z2* symmetry under an additional phase condition, whereas the Higgs-family D8 potential automatically has D8 × Z2* symmetry when antiunitary transformations are included. These statements concern scalar potentials and do not establish a complete fermion-sector construction or a vacuum symmetry-breaking pattern.','Complete for the seed''s classification-domain and two-construction scope. D6 and D8 were chosen as the first two explicit nonabelian constructions in the paper; their CP extensions are recorded separately. Other classified groups are listed but their potentials are deferred.','2026-10-04T03:37:16+00:00','P08-20261004T033027Z-a716b537','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(9,'hep-ph/0606172v3',1,'The paper studies the Standard Model supplemented by one color-octet electroweak-doublet scalar S^A in (8,2)1/2, with charged and neutral components. Its quark Yukawa couplings are aligned with the Standard Model Yukawa matrices, under a symmetry-based naturalness assumption whose limitations are discussed in Appendix A. The paper supplies the renormalizable scalar potential, electroweak mass splittings, and additional CP-violating phases, and separately describes a custodial-symmetry restriction. Scalar loops can substantially modify gluon-fusion Higgs production and generate flavor and neutron-electric-dipole effects. An optional unification extension adds three real weak triplet scalars; conflicting representation labels elsewhere in the text require curation.','Complete for the assigned scalar-construction scope, including the optional triplet extension and the qualification of the MFV argument. Detailed production cross sections, flavor Wilson coefficients, and neutron matrix-element estimates are contextual results rather than a full phenomenological extraction.','2026-10-04T03:44:39+00:00','P09-20261004T033717Z-5a9b6a4e','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(10,'1306.0572v3',1,'The paper considers seven separate minimal extensions of the SM: T and B singlets, (X T), (T B), and (B Y) doublets, and (X T B) and (T B Y) triplets. Its classification assumes renormalisable mixing and a scalar sector containing only SU(2)L doublets, with one vector-like multiplet included at a time and predominant third-generation coupling implemented through t–T and b–B mixing. In the paper''s weak-basis mass matrices, singlets and triplets have y43^q = 0, doublets have y34^q = 0, and the triplet up/down Yukawas obey fixed √2 relations. Biunitary diagonalisation distinguishes the weak fields and bare mass M^0 from physical quarks, masses, and gauge/Higgs couplings; left- and right-handed angles are related by light-to-heavy mass ratios. All choices have one independent mixing angle except the (T B) doublet, which has two, while multiplet component masses obey correlated splitting relations.','Complete for the seven-multiplet classification and mixing scope. Quantitative electroweak fits, production rates, branching ratios, and the complete Appendix A coupling tables are not reproduced.','2026-10-04T03:52:05+00:00','P10-20261004T034440Z-f81bf431','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(11,'1603.04993v3',1,'The review classifies six scalar and six vector leptoquark multiplets, including states whose lepton couplings require right-chiral neutrinos, and specifies their renormalizable fermion interactions. It distinguishes genuine leptoquarks without diquark couplings from representations that can mediate proton decay, while qualifying baryon- and lepton-number assignments by the operator content and field content. Its massive-vector interaction descriptions require additional ultraviolet information: a representation and explicit mass alone do not define a renormalizable gauge theory. Representative completion discussions include SU(5) scalar extensions, an SO(10) scalar embedding and Pati–Salam-related gauge constructions, with their stated proton-stability restrictions kept separate from the general interaction taxonomy.','Complete for the selective seed scope: representation and interaction taxonomy, B/L assumptions, and representative completion distinctions. This is not an exhaustive extraction of every model or experimental result reviewed. Text ambiguities and details delegated by the review to original references remain explicitly unresolved.','2026-10-04T06:00:31+00:00','P11-20261004T054649Z-4d15039d','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(12,'hep-ph/0512090v4',1,'The paper classifies colorless, minimally non-chiral spin-0 and spin-1/2 electroweak multiplets containing a neutral component, with gauge interactions and a common tree-level mass as its minimal starting point. Its accidental-stability argument depends on the absence of dangerous low-dimension decay operators: section 2 identifies n ≥ 5 for fermions and n ≥ 7 for scalars, while requiring sufficiently suppressed higher-dimension operators. Table 1 includes the fermion quintuplet and scalar septuplet with Y = 0, a scalar quintuplet with a dangerous dimension-5 decay, and smaller multiplets requiring additional stabilization. Scalar Higgs couplings are allowed but assumed small in the minimal numerical scenarios, while nonzero-hypercharge candidates require extra structure to evade the paper''s direct-detection constraints. The quoted masses, including 4.4 TeV for the fermion quintuplet and 8.5 TeV for the scalar septuplet, follow from a single-multiplet thermal-relic scenario with coannihilations; the pinned version explicitly warns that later nonperturbative corrections substantially affect this perturbative abundance calculation.','Complete for the assigned classification scope. All Table 1 field assignments and dangerous-decay labels are captured. Detection forecasts, collider event counts, and detailed indirect-detection calculations are not exhaustively extracted.','2026-10-04T06:11:20+00:00','P12-20261004T060032Z-3e827093','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(13,'0801.1345v3',1,'The review establishes conventions for additional Abelian currents, charge normalization, scalar-induced neutral gauge-boson masses, and kinetic mixing. It distinguishes anomaly cancellation from charge orthogonality and explains why family-universal extensions usually require additional fermions. Selected explicit charge assignments comprise pure B−L with right-handed neutrinos and the E6-inspired χ and ψ benchmarks, whose exotic content has different chirality properties. Separately scoped symmetry-breaking examples describe a single-singlet supersymmetric sector, a secluded sector, and a flat-direction toy model; the review also discusses Stückelberg masses without a Higgs VEV.','Complete for the selective seed scope, not an exhaustive extraction of this review. Charge benchmarks and symmetry-breaking examples are separate descriptions and are not assembled into a common completion. Collider constraints and most phenomenological applications are deferred.','2026-10-04T06:20:57+00:00','P13-20261004T061121Z-35f0a566','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(14,'0812.4313v1',1,'The paper defines a pure minimal SU(3)C × SU(2)L × U(1)Y × U(1)B−L extension with three right-handed neutrinos of B−L charge −1 and a complex singlet χ of charge +2 in addition to the Standard Model Higgs doublet. It chooses diagonal Abelian kinetic terms and a covariant derivative with no mixed Abelian coupling, and explicitly assumes no Z–Z′ mixing. The χ vacuum expectation value generates right-handed Majorana masses, while the Higgs doublet generates Dirac neutrino masses, producing three light and three heavy Majorana neutrinos through a seesaw mass matrix. The collider implementation neglects intergenerational neutrino mixing and takes generation-degenerate masses; its chosen Z′, neutrino, and scalar benchmarks are separate restrictions on the underlying construction. The study emphasizes Z′ production followed by heavy-neutrino pairs, including a three-lepton signal and the possibility of inferring a light-neutrino mass from a heavy-neutrino mass and displaced decay.','Complete for the assigned construction and convention-extraction scope. Detailed cut efficiencies, all plotted branching fractions, and a full scalar-sector analysis are outside this extraction; reconciliation with another paper is reserved for the curator.','2026-10-04T06:28:46+00:00','P14-20261004T062058Z-2b12d981','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(15,'1812.04602v5',1,'The paper classifies family-dependent U(1)F fermion charges for the SM and the SM with up to three right-handed neutrinos using six anomaly cancellation equations. It assumes rational charges and identifies assignments under overall rescaling and independent family permutations within each fermion species, using ordered primitive integer representatives for enumeration. Its bounded searches reach Qmax = 10 and report 23,800 SM and 21,546,920 SMνR equivalence classes, including the all-zero assignment. Analytic results cover restricted families of solutions, while a separate Yukawa analysis requires universal electrically charged fermion charges when every SM Yukawa matrix entry is allowed. The selected explicit assignments are partial constructions: anomaly freedom and occasional Higgs-charge or mixing assumptions do not specify complete scalar, symmetry-breaking or fermion-mass sectors.','Summary covers classification assumptions, equations, equivalences, finite enumeration and selected examples. Counts are properties of the bounded classification, not numbers of complete physical models.','2026-10-04T06:36:20+00:00','P15-20261004T062847Z-99614372','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(16,'hep-ph/9206242v1',1,'The paper constructs an SU_L(3) ⊗ U_N(1) electroweak theory with color-counted quark multiplets and electric charge Q/e = (λ3 − √3λ8)/2 + N. Each lepton family occupies (ν_l, l, l^c)_L in (3,0), while the first quark family is a triplet and the other two are antitriplets containing exotic quarks of charges +5/3 and −4/3. Three scalar triplets η, ρ and χ generate hierarchical symmetry breaking, with χ setting the exotic-quark and heavy-vector mass scale. The authors omit right-handed neutrinos, take neutrinos to be massless, and use a nonstandard charge-conjugation prescription in deriving charged-lepton masses. They describe explicit lepton-number violation through η2+ and the charged vectors V and U, but find no η1−–η2− mixing or neutrinoless double beta decay in their minimal scalar construction. They also outline a separate extension with two additional η-like triplets that permits the charged-scalar mixing.','Complete for the assigned construction and its explicitly outlined scalar extension using all supplied pages. Claims reproduce this pinned version''s assumptions and conventions; later revisions, alternative 331 constructions and independent consistency checks are outside this extraction.','2026-10-04T06:44:02+00:00','P16-20261004T063621Z-e90e00e2','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(17,'1307.6213v3',1,'The paper constructs quark–lepton unification with GQL = SU(4)C ⊗ SU(2)L ⊗ U(1)R, placing quarks and leptons in common multiplets and breaking to the Standard Model through a fundamental scalar χ. Its scalar sector also contains H and an adjoint doublet Φ, whose components include a second Higgs doublet, a color octet doublet, and two scalar leptoquark doublets. Independent Yukawa matrices avoid the naive charged-fermion mass relations, while adding three singlet fermions N permits naturally small Majorana neutrino masses through an inverse seesaw. In the assumed regime without flavor suppression, rare meson decays require vector leptoquark masses of order 10^3 TeV or higher, while scalar leptoquarks may remain at the TeV scale. The paper gives a neutrino-mass benchmark linked to this hierarchy and assumes a vacuum preserving color and electric charge.','Complete for the assigned construction and its linked regimes. The minimal Dirac-neutrino construction and its inverse-seesaw extension are distinguished; proposed ultraviolet completions and supersymmetrization are left as undeveloped possibilities.','2026-10-04T06:51:24+00:00','P17-20261004T064402Z-200b3144','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(18,'hep-ph/0702287v4',1,'The paper presents a renormalizable, non-supersymmetric SU(5) construction with ordinary matter in 5̄ and 10 and one additional fermionic adjoint 24. Its Higgs sector is 5H + 24H + 45H: the adjoint VEV breaks the unified symmetry, while 5H and 45H supply the stated fermion-mass Yukawa interactions. The adjoint fermion contains a triplet and singlet that generate type III and type I seesaw contributions, yielding one massless neutrino at tree level, and its component masses follow from a bare mass and coupling to 24H. The paper gives conditional one-loop unification benchmarks, including low unification scales requiring proton-decay suppression and higher scales obtained with light scalar thresholds. It also proposes leptogenesis through out-of-equilibrium decays of the adjoint triplet and singlet, leaving its detailed demonstration to future work.','Complete for the assigned seed scope using all supplied pages. Representation overbars and a few equation layouts remain text-extraction ambiguities; the scalar potential and detailed leptogenesis calculation are not supplied by this paper.','2026-10-04T07:00:07+00:00','P18-20261004T065125Z-6f17c67e','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(19,'1202.0807v1',1,'The paper specifies the general renormalizable potential of a non-supersymmetric SO(10) breaking sector containing 45H and 126H, with three SM-singlet vacuum directions ωBL, ωR and σ. The adjoint VEV alignments allow left-right, SU(4)C, two-U(1), and standard or flipped SU(5) intermediate symmetries; a nonzero σ generally completes breaking to the SM except for the standard SU(5) alignment. Tree-level pseudo-Goldstone masses obstruct the phenomenologically interesting hierarchical adjoint VEVs, and the paper uses a positive universal τ² one-loop correction to obtain a minimally regularized spectrum. Within this approximation, accidental (6,3,+1/3) or (8,2,+1/2) thresholds support unification with a substantially raised B − L scale. The 45H ⊕ 126H sector alone cannot reproduce fermion flavor; the paper separately proposes a potentially realistic 45H ⊕ 126H ⊕ 10H extension and explains the additional Yukawa and Higgs-mixing requirements.','Complete for the assigned breaking-sector scope and its distinction from a fermion-mass completion. Numerical unification results are recorded as conditional on the paper''s approximate spectrum and one-loop running, rather than as independently validated full-model predictions.','2026-10-04T07:08:24+00:00','P19-20261004T070009Z-02a3e9ec','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(20,'1507.06848v2',1,'The paper constructs weak-scale SU(3)L ⊗ SU(3)R ⊗ SU(3)c theories with three chiral families and argues that three scalar bi-triplets permit experimentally acceptable exotic-fermion masses without the severe fermion-mass tuning of two-bi-triplet models. A representative extension adds one Majorana 8L fermion coupled through 8L LH*, for which the authors report total asymptotic freedom at one loop, requiring nonzero asymptotic fixed-point Yukawa couplings for lighter generations. The bi-triplet vacuum expectation values break trinification to the Standard Model and then to electromagnetism, while adjoint fermions can generate sterile-neutrino Majorana masses. The paper classifies alternative unstable and stable extra-fermion choices and checks that its stated two-loop strong-coupling correction permits the physical coupling in the examined range. These are existence and classification results: explicit weak-scale predictions require RG evolution and potential minimisation beyond the paper.','Complete for the assigned construction and variant-classification scope using all supplied pages. Numerical fixed flows, complete beta-function systems and detailed vacuum solutions cannot be reconstructed from this PDF alone. Ambiguous extracted formulas are identified rather than corrected.','2026-10-04T07:18:18+00:00','P20-20261004T070825Z-6ec4111d','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(21,'hep-ph/9709356v7',1,'The review defines the MSSM through its chiral superfields, Standard Model gauge interactions, a holomorphic Yukawa superpotential with an independent μ term, and exact matter parity or equivalently R-parity. Its general conventional soft Lagrangian allows independent gaugino masses, complex trilinear matrices, Hermitian sfermion squared-mass matrices, and Higgs soft parameters. Flavor universality, proportional trilinears, restricted CP phases, and unified soft parameters are additional assumptions, with Planck-scale, gauge-mediated, and anomaly-mediated scenarios treated separately. The electroweak analysis assumes vanishing squark and slepton VEVs and a neutral two-Higgs vacuum, derives tree-level stability and minimization conditions, and explains the need for radiative corrections. The full introductory representation tables were not supplied, so the field-assignment extraction remains incomplete.','This is a source-specific extraction of the common MSSM definition and selected restricted soft-parameter scenarios. Missing introductory field tables prevent complete coverage of the requested gauge representations; comparisons to other papers and canonical model identity are left to curation.','2026-10-04T07:29:54+00:00','P21-20261004T071819Z-40c8d6b1','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(22,'hep-ph/0406039v2',1,'The review presents R-parity violation with MSSM field content through the bilinear μ_i H_u L_i and trilinear L_i L_j E_k^c, L_i Q_j D_k^c and U_i^c D_j^c D_k^c superpotential operators, separating lepton-number from baryon-number violation. It supplies the associated soft terms and stresses that Higgs–lepton basis choices change the apparent bilinear and trilinear parameters without removing physical mixing. Explicit bilinear, tree-level trilinear, soft-only and spontaneous breaking patterns have different restrictions and must be kept separate. A singlet-extended spontaneous-breaking example preserves R-parity and lepton number in its superpotential but generates effective bilinears through singlet sneutrino VEVs. Flavour symmetries, unified gauge groups and generalized matter, baryon and lepton parities impose additional, scenario-dependent operator restrictions.','Selective structural extraction, not a catalogue of all phenomenology in this 263-page review. Coverage is partial because the full field-definition appendix and bibliography were unavailable; the main operator and symmetry discussion on supplied pages 24–51 was inspected.','2026-10-04T07:41:17+00:00','P22-20261004T072957Z-ce0794fc','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(23,'hep-ph/0406088v2',1,'The paper studies Split Supersymmetry with squarks, sleptons, and the extra Higgs states at a heavy scale m̃, while retaining Standard Model particles, one tuned light Higgs doublet, gauginos, and two higgsino doublets below that threshold. Its matter-parity-preserving renormalizable effective interactions are matched to a supersymmetric parent through β-dependent boundary conditions for the Higgs quartic, Yukawa couplings, and Higgs–higgsino–gaugino couplings. Below m̃, nonsupersymmetric running changes these coupling relations and mixes the electroweak gaugino masses with μ. Gauge unification remains viable in the paper''s threshold analysis, but unknown high-scale corrections prevent firm parameter bounds. Requiring neutralinos to supply the dark matter selects conditional mixed bino–higgsino, approximately 1.0–1.2 TeV higgsino, or approximately 2.0–2.5 TeV wino regions, rather than fixing a universal fermion mass scale.','Complete for the assigned construction, parent matching, and conditional unification/dark-matter scope. Numerical figures, full RGE transcription, alternative nonsupersymmetric inventories, and detailed collider and detection forecasts are not exhaustively extracted.','2026-10-04T07:48:02+00:00','P23-20261004T074117Z-21a9ea21','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(24,'hep-ph/0206096v3',1,'The paper''s Gauge Extended Models add an adjoint chiral multiplet for each Standard Model gauge factor, forming an N = 2 gauge sector while retaining N = 1 chiral matter. A hidden U(1)'' D-term generates a supersoft operator that pairs each gaugino with an adjoint fermion and simultaneously supplies real-adjoint scalar masses and scalar trilinear interactions absent from the MSSM. These linked interactions give finite, flavor-blind gauge contributions to scalar masses and, in the minimal limit, eliminate low-energy D-term quartics. D-term breaking preserves R symmetry, but the construction also assumes supersoft dominance; supersymmetric adjoint masses are optional, and no symmetry permits the Dirac operator while excluding the second supersoft adjoint-bilinear operator. Heavy messenger thresholds, fundamental cutoff-suppressed interactions, and extra-dimensional arrangements are proposed as distinct possible origins rather than a unique ultraviolet completion.','Complete for the assigned gauge-sector, breaking-source, operator, symmetry-assumption and ultraviolet-origin scope. Detailed benchmark spectra, Higgs phenomenology and cosmology are deferred.','2026-10-04T07:51:04+00:00','P24-20261004T074803Z-7f9e42b7','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(25,'0801.3278v3',1,'The paper defines general gauge mediation through decoupling of the MSSM and a supersymmetry-breaking hidden sector as the MSSM gauge couplings vanish, encompassing weakly coupled messengers and strongly coupled direct mediation. Hidden-sector current correlators determine leading gaugino masses, sfermion masses and gauge thresholds without specifying the hidden dynamics, and do not enforce gaugino mass unification. At the mediation scale, two sfermion sum rules hold per generation when the hypercharge current expectation value vanishes; a nonzero expectation value leaves one relation, with running and Higgs-sector qualifications. Preliminary direct Higgs couplings extend the framework and express the µ/Bµ problem in terms of additional hidden-sector correlators without supplying a complete solution. Appendix A explicitly realizes the formalism in a U(1) minimal messenger toy model with a prescribed supersymmetry-breaking spurion.','Complete for the assigned classification scope. General parameterizations and proposed Higgs extensions are distinguished from the explicit messenger construction; none is promoted to a complete ultraviolet hidden-sector theory.','2026-10-04T07:59:10+00:00','P25-20261004T075104Z-c0c293f7','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(26,'2003.01100v4',1,'The review develops benchmark KSVZ and DFSZ ultraviolet constructions of the QCD axion and fixes the anomaly convention through f_a = v_a/(2N). Its simplest KSVZ construction adds a hypercharge-neutral vector-like colour triplet and a complex singlet, with explicit PQ charges and a singlet-generated fermion mass. DFSZ-I and DFSZ-II share two Higgs doublets and a singlet but differ in the charged-lepton Yukawa assignment, yielding E/N = 8/3 and 2/3 respectively. The review specifies the physical DFSZ axion direction, the scalar PQ-charge normalization, and the standard domain-wall number six; replacing the quartic singlet interaction with a cubic interaction is a distinct choice with domain-wall number three. It also emphasizes that the benchmark constructions do not themselves resolve the PQ-quality problem.','The benchmark physics is extracted from the supplied sections; coverage is partial because the requested original-source provenance could not be checked against the bibliography. Additional constructions are listed for later extraction.','2026-10-04T08:08:08+00:00','P26-20261004T075914Z-c1038266','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(27,'1610.01639v2',1,'The authors'' SMASH construction adds a complex singlet, three singlet neutrinos and one vector-like colored fermion, connecting seesaw masses and a QCD axion through a shared PQ/lepton symmetry. Its scalar portal and non-minimal gravitational couplings support inflation and threshold stabilization, with the authors favoring mixed Higgs–hidden-scalar inflation after considering reheating and dark radiation. Their combined cosmology uses PQ restoration, reheating near 10¹⁰ GeV, post-inflationary axion production and thermal leptogenesis, with mildly degenerate heavy neutrinos easing the tension between leptogenesis and singlet stability. The main dark-matter estimate is fA = vσ ≈ 3×10¹⁰–1.2×10¹¹ GeV, with acknowledged defect-production uncertainties and an inconsistent upper limit elsewhere in the same version. The title''s five-problem solution is recorded as an author claim supported by linked mechanisms and parameter estimates; a fully specified common benchmark with a computed baryon yield remains unresolved in the inspected source.','Source-specific extraction of the main down-type SMASH construction and its HHSI, HSI and non-restored-PQ regimes. Statements are historical claims of the pinned version, not present-day observational validation. Complete extraction coverage does not imply independent confirmation of the combined viability claim.','2026-10-04T08:19:38+00:00','P27-20261004T080810Z-49f55d3b','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(28,'hep-ph/9212278v1',1,'The paper constructs a supersymmetric horizontal-symmetry example with two singlets, family-dependent U(1)H1 × U(1)H2 charges, and expansion parameters ϵ1 ∼ 0.04 and ϵ2 ∼ 0.008. Charge-balanced holomorphic effective Yukawa operators generate approximate quark textures, with two up-sector zeros in the continuous-charge description and the desired mass and mixing hierarchies for comparable Higgs VEVs. An explicit heavy realization contains three vectorlike U pairs and five vectorlike D pairs, although the scalar vacuum and complete discrete-symmetry realization are left open. Under the paper''s Planck-scale perturbativity criterion this eight-pair realization has a high heavy-fermion threshold; a separate variant with mu = 0 and a small Higgs-VEV ratio reduces the heavy content to one U pair and two D pairs and supports the proposed possibility of much lower flavor scales.','Complete for the selected explicit quark construction and its principal comparisons. This is a source extraction, not independent validation of the numerical phenomenology, ultraviolet consistency, or present-day viability.','2026-10-04T08:28:12+00:00','P28-20261004T081939Z-e9b25854','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(29,'hep-ph/0512103v2',1,'The paper constructs a four-dimensional supersymmetric lepton model with A4 × Z3 × U(1)F × U(1)R, separating charged-lepton and neutrino flavons through their auxiliary charges. A driving superpotential and a second singlet support the alignments ⟨ϕT⟩ = (vT,0,0), ⟨ϕS⟩ = (vS,vS,vS), ⟨ξ⟩ = u and ⟨ξ̃⟩ = 0, subject to soft-mass choices and assumed stabilization of flat directions. The effective-operator realization produces diagonal charged-lepton masses and leading-order tri-bimaximal neutrino mixing; higher-dimensional terms perturb the alignment and motivate a common flavon-VEV/cutoff interval of roughly 0.0022–0.05. A distinct seesaw realization introduces an A4 triplet ν^c and changes several Z3 charges while retaining the driving sector and leading mixing pattern. The modular-group discussion supplies a representation-theoretic connection, with a dynamical origin left speculative; internal field-assignment and Dirac-matrix inconsistencies remain flagged.','Complete for the requested construction and its seesaw variant, including leading interactions, auxiliary symmetry actions, vacuum assumptions, and the structure of alignment corrections. Detailed modular representation classification, quark model building, and coefficient-by-coefficient higher-order phenomenology are not separately extracted.','2026-10-04T08:34:49+00:00','P29-20261004T082813Z-de459784','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(30,'1706.08749v2',1,'The selected construction is the flavon-free N = 1 supersymmetric type-I seesaw example at modular level 3, with Γ3 ≅ A4, triplet L and N^c, and three differently assigned charged-lepton singlets. Matter transformation weights and a weight-2 modular-form triplet fix the charged-lepton, neutrino Dirac, and Majorana superpotential couplings. The modulus vacuum is scanned as a spurion rather than derived from a stabilization potential; the reported benchmark gives inverted ordering but experimentally excluded mixing angles. Compared with ordinary supersymmetric A4 constructions, the additional modular automorphy factors, weight matching, holomorphic coupling functions, and relation Y2² + 2Y1Y3 = 0 restrict both interactions and alignments. The paper''s claim of a completely determined lepton superpotential applies in the exact supersymmetric regime, with corrections possible through supersymmetry breaking or the Kähler potential.','Complete for the requested extraction of one explicitly specified example and this paper''s comparison with ordinary A4 constructions. Other lepton examples and the general orbit-space analysis are deferred.','2026-10-04T08:40:00+00:00','P30-20261004T083450Z-e513088e','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(31,'1009.2049v3',1,'The paper constructs a renormalizable quark flavor gauge theory, principally with SU(3)QL × SU(3)UR × SU(3)DR × U(1)², two bifundamental flavons, and exotic quarks whose representations are motivated by the authors'' anomaly-cancellation argument. Universal Higgs couplings and mass mixings yield effective Yukawa matrices proportional to inverse flavon VEVs in the heavy-partner limit, while exact mixing relations are needed for the top sector. The resulting inverted hierarchy suppresses light-generation flavor transitions through large mediator masses, with additional Yukawa suppression for flavon couplings; the construction differs from MFV because flavor breaking can remain when the Yukawas vanish. The observed flavon vacuum is assumed: two bifundamentals alone cannot produce it with a renormalizable potential, motivating higher-dimensional potentials or additional bifundamentals. Separate SU(3)³ and SU(3)³ × U(1)² numerical examples illustrate the authors'' claimed compatibility with then-current constraints and contain lightest flavor vectors of 2.8 TeV and 0.29 TeV, respectively.','Summary covers the general construction and distinguishes its assumptions from the two illustrative parameter choices. Collider searches, oblique-loop formulas and complete numerical four-quark coefficient tables are not exhaustively catalogued.','2026-10-04T08:46:23+00:00','P31-20261004T084001Z-f2b36897','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(32,'hep-ph/0505013v1',1,'The paper defines the νMSM as the N = 3, electroweak-scale-or-lighter realization of the renormalisable right-handed singlet-neutrino extension, with neutrino Yukawa interactions and Majorana masses. Its studied regime has Majorana masses much larger than Dirac masses, very small Yukawa couplings, and a keV lightest sterile neutrino whose dark-matter abundance restricts its coupling and mixing with the heavier states. The authors present an existence argument for simultaneously accommodating neutrino oscillations, dark matter and baryon asymmetry under standard Big Bang evolution, initially absent sterile neutrinos and initially vanishing lepton-flavour asymmetries. Their baryogenesis treatment includes active-flavour asymmetries and their transfer back to sterile neutrinos, followed by sphaleron conversion of the compensating active-sector asymmetry. In the perturbative normal-hierarchy example, approximately 10 GeV heavier sterile neutrinos require strong mass degeneracy, of order one part in 10^6, with suitable CP violation; these are conditions of the demonstrated scenario rather than atlas selection requirements.','Complete for the assigned field-content, interaction, hierarchy and shared phenomenological-assumption scope. The paper demonstrates a restricted perturbative realization rather than surveying the complete νMSM parameter space. Source-family relationships are recorded descriptively without canonical model identity decisions or paper–model links.','2026-10-04T08:52:29+00:00','P32-20261004T084624Z-170718f2','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(33,'hep-ph/0601225v1',1,'The paper extends the Standard Model by three neutral Majorana fermion singlets N_i and a scalar doublet η, all odd under an exact Z2 while the displayed Standard Model fields are even. In the assumed vacuum only φ0 has a nonzero expectation value, so the forbidden Φ†η bilinear and vanishing η0 expectation value prevent a tree-level neutrino Dirac mass. The h_ij lepton–η–N interaction, Majorana masses and λ5(Φ†η)^2 interaction generate light Majorana neutrino masses at one loop, with the neutral scalar splitting entering the loop result. The lightest odd particle is stable, allowing separate fermionic N1 and neutral-scalar dark-matter realizations with different illustrative mass hierarchies and decay chains. These shared doublet and singlet ingredients are recorded together with their interactions and symmetry assumptions for later comparison, without assigning identity to constructions in other papers.','Complete for the requested field content, parity, interactions, vacuum, radiative mechanism and separate dark-matter realizations. Optional flavor-symmetry implementations and external comparator theories are deferred.','2026-10-04T08:56:37+00:00','P33-20261004T085230Z-8fa30bc0','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(34,'1204.5862v2',1,'The paper classifies one-loop completions of the d = 5 Weinberg operator using scalar and fermion mediators, excluding self-energy insertions, and separates finite irreducible diagrams, finite seesaw extensions, and divergent seesaw corrections. The irreducible T1 and T3 families can furnish leading neutrino masses, with a Z2 symmetry used when tree-level seesaw mediators are present. Generic finite T4 extensions still allow tree-level seesaws; the paper obtains genuinely leading T4-2-i and T4-3-i contributions by combining Majorana fermions in the loop, lepton-number-conserving couplings, and a symmetry preventing additional scalar vacuum expectation values. These yield a loop-induced type-II Yukawa coupling or a linear-seesaw contribution, while the inverse-seesaw mass insertion requires a self-energy diagram outside the enumeration. Divergent T4, T5, and T6 diagrams require tree-level seesaw counterterms and are interpreted as vertex corrections. The listed field assignments and necessary interactions describe operator completions and diagrammatic families, without specifying complete scalar potentials or fully defined models.','Complete for the assigned classification scope, with bounded examples rather than a transcription of every representation row or flavor-indexed mass formula. Ambiguous representation entries are explicitly flagged.','2026-10-04T09:02:17+00:00','P34-20261004T085637Z-ab50204c','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(35,'1404.7137v1',1,'The paper specifies an asymptotically free SU(4) hypercolor theory with five left-handed Weyl fermions in the antisymmetric 6 and three each in the 4 and 4̄. It argues for condensates breaking the flavor symmetry to SO(5) × SU(3)c × U(1)X, yielding an SU(5)/SO(5) Higgs sector, a color-octet pNGB and an additional singlet. Three-hyperfermion operators supply candidate top partners, with one light Dirac (5,3)2/3 multiplet assumed in the IR. Linear elementary–composite mixing generates the top mass, while quadratic pNGB couplings provide the other fermion masses. The proposed electroweak vacuum follows from top-driven Higgs misalignment with color preserved, but the necessary strong dynamics and higher-scale interaction origin remain unproved.','Complete for the assigned seed scope, with separate related records for the microscopic theory, retained IR effective theory and vacuum assumptions. Detailed heavy-resonance spectroscopy and a phenomenological recast of the quoted experimental bounds are deferred.','2026-10-04T09:09:07+00:00','P35-20261004T090218Z-5c059ead','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(36,'hep-ph/0206021v2',1,'The paper constructs a little Higgs theory from SU(5)/SO(5), gauging [SU(2) × U(1)]² and retaining a light electroweak Higgs doublet after four Goldstones are eaten and a complex triplet becomes massive. Gauge interactions and a two-coupling top sector collectively break the protecting symmetries, with heavy gauge bosons, a scalar triplet and a charge-2/3 quark cancelling the leading one-loop quadratic sensitivities. Integrating out the triplet generates a Higgs quartic, while the negative logarithmic top-sector contribution can trigger electroweak symmetry breaking. The construction assumes f of order a TeV and a cutoff near 4πf, with UV-sensitive potential coefficients rather than a fixed numerical benchmark. A separately described top-sector alternative uses complete SU(5) fermion multiplets and soft symmetry breaking to reduce the UV sensitivity of the quartic.','Complete for the assigned minimal-model scope using the supplied text. UV completions and enlarged cosets are identified as deferred variants; numerical naturalness illustrations are distinguished from defining assumptions.','2026-10-04T09:15:52+00:00','P36-20261004T090908Z-1a41a805','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(37,'hep-ph/0506256v2',1,'The paper constructs twin Higgs theories with two Standard Model gauge sectors and a discrete symmetry relating their matter and interactions, distinguishing direct twin interchange from spatial parity combined with interchange to CP-conjugate mirror particles. A Higgs sector with approximate SU(4), broken to SU(3), supplies a pseudo-Goldstone Higgs; the interchange symmetry forces leading quadratically divergent mass corrections into a globally invariant form. The nonlinear realization assumes O(8) symmetry to suppress custodial violation, while a soft μ²H_A†H_A term permits a hierarchy between electroweak breaking and f. The paper separates twin models with an SU(6) × SU(4) × U(1) extended top sector from twin models without that extension and from mirror-symmetric models. Higgs and hypercharge mixing portals connect the sectors, and the presence of exotic quarks in the extended top construction changes the kinetic-mixing constraints and motivates a heavy twin photon.','Summary and assertions cover the assigned construction and symmetry scope across all five supplied pages. The distinct twin and mirror transformations, extended and unextended top sectors, and massive and potentially massless twin-photon cases are retained separately. Numerical tuning benchmarks and detailed cosmological analysis are outside this scoped extraction.','2026-10-04T09:21:38+00:00','P37-20261004T091552Z-b62d7418','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(38,'1501.05310v2',1,'The paper constructs a Fraternal Twin Higgs sector containing a twin Higgs doublet, third-generation twin fermions, and gauged twin SU(2) and SU(3), with all twin particles neutral under Standard Model gauge interactions. An approximately SU(4)-symmetric scalar potential and closely matched top Yukawa and gauge couplings protect the pseudo-Goldstone Higgs, while soft exchange-symmetry breaking permits v ≪ f with modest electroweak tuning. Compared with the original mirror construction as described here, the minimal model omits first- and second-generation twin fermions and low-energy twin hypercharge, and does not require matching of small Yukawa couplings. Twin color confines into glueballs and, where relevant, twin bottomonium; these are infrared descriptions of the same microscopic sector. Higgs mixing and twin-top loops connect scalar twin hadrons to the visible sector, allowing exotic Higgs decays and potentially displaced hadron decays, with substantial nonperturbative uncertainty in production and bottomonium phenomenology.','Complete for the assigned minimal construction, its naturalness assumptions, microscopic-to-infrared connection, and this paper''s side of the original-model comparison. Detailed collider searches and nonminimal model variants are deferred.','2026-10-04T09:24:51+00:00','P38-20261004T092139Z-ec4f3b83','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(39,'hep-ph/9803315v1',1,'The paper proposes n ≥ 2 compact extra spatial dimensions with a fundamental gravitational scale M_Pl(4+n) ∼ m_EW and effective four-dimensional gravity obeying M_Pl² ∼ M_Pl(4+n)^(2+n) R^n. Its low-energy content consists of localized Standard Model fields and a bulk graviton; n = 2 gives the highlighted compactification range R ∼ 100 μm–1 mm. A separate illustrative construction realizes the four-dimensional world as the core of a vortex in six dimensions, with sphere and zero-inner-radius torus compactifications discussed. That construction has bulk Pati-Salam symmetry, core-localized symmetry breaking and chiral matter, and relies crucially on an assumed bulk gauge mass gap near the cutoff. The origin of the large compactification size remains uncalculated, and the paper''s rough supernova estimate favors n > 2 or m_EW > 10 TeV.','Complete for the requested dimensional, gravitational and localization scope, including the separate six-dimensional Pati-Salam specification. Phenomenological estimates are selective and reflect only this pinned version.','2026-10-04T09:29:44+00:00','P39-20261004T092452Z-550d9db8','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(40,'hep-ph/9905221v1',1,'The paper constructs a five-dimensional gravitational theory on S^1/Z_2 with a finite interval bounded by hidden and visible 3-branes, with Standard Model fields on the visible brane. A four-dimensional Poincare-invariant warped background is a slice of AdS_5 and requires a negative bulk cosmological constant and tuned, oppositely signed brane vacuum energies. Reduction gives a massless four-dimensional graviton with M_Pl^2 = (M^3/k)(1 − e^{−2k r_c π}), while visible-brane mass parameters are reduced by e^{−k r_c π}. The radius is a modulus whose stabilization remains unresolved, and the authors assume it fixed when deriving the effective theory. Massive gravitational Kaluza-Klein excitations are stated to have weak-scale masses and couplings, but their explicit decomposition is left to future work.','Complete for the assigned scope using all nine supplied pages. Detailed massive-mode spectra, radius stabilization and ultraviolet completion are not provided by this version.','2026-10-04T09:34:12+00:00','P40-20261004T092945Z-41d58bfe','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(41,'hep-ph/0012100v2',1,'The paper constructs an effective Standard Model in d = 4 + δ dimensions, with all Standard Model fields propagating in the compact dimensions and a cutoff Ms above 1/R. Its orbifold boundary conditions select the chiral Standard Model zero-modes, while nonzero KK levels repeat the bulk fields as four-dimensional vector-like fermions, gauge fields, a Higgs doublet and extra-dimensional gauge components. The paper derives the KK spectrum, top-sector mixing and interactions and treats the minimal bulk construction as conserving KK number, thereby forbidding vertices with a single nonzero KK mode. One, two and three-or-more universal dimensions have distinct ultraviolet sensitivity: the one-dimensional one-loop sums converge, the two-dimensional sums depend logarithmically on the cutoff, and higher-dimensional estimates have stronger cutoff dependence. It also discusses separate KK-number-violating extensions involving a thick brane or localized operators; its numerical bounds are historical, assumption-dependent results of this paper rather than current exclusions.','Complete for the requested construction scope using all supplied pages. Detailed electroweak loop formulas and collider limits are represented only where needed to explain ultraviolet assumptions, selection rules and scoped variants. No canonical model identity or paper–model association is assigned.','2026-10-04T09:42:25+00:00','P41-20261004T093413Z-8d2eaf37','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(42,'hep-ph/0306259v1',1,'The paper constructs a Higgs sector in a slice of five-dimensional anti-de Sitter space whose holographic description contains a strongly coupled conformal sector with a composite pseudo-Goldstone Higgs. Its explicit SU(3)L × U(1)X model breaks the symmetry on both branes, identifies the Higgs with the doublet in an infrared-brane scalar, and obtains Yukawa interactions from bulk fermions coupled to that scalar. When symmetry breaking is imposed through boundary conditions, the surviving pseudo-Goldstone modes instead become zero modes of A5, with profiles concentrated toward the infrared brane. Gauge and fermion interactions generate finite nonlocal contributions to the Higgs potential, with a negative top contribution capable of triggering electroweak breaking. A sizable tree-level quartic requires additional structure: the paper proposes a bulk scalar mediator with a specific ultraviolet mass-splitting pattern, and also discusses supersymmetric gauge quartics and a modified mediator mechanism for A5 realizations.','Complete for the assigned construction, symmetry breaking, Higgs identification, interactions and quartic scope. The extraction distinguishes the explicit brane-scalar construction, the general A5 construction, the outlined SU(6) realization and the nonsupersymmetric A5 quartic extension; it does not supply external implementation details.','2026-10-04T09:49:57+00:00','P42-20261004T094226Z-24f457ea','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(43,'hep-ph/0604261v2',1,'The paper presents a Hidden Valley family in which Standard Model neutral constituents confine into hidden hadrons and communicate with Standard Model particles through heavy mediators. Its explicit U(1)χ × SU(nv) construction contains two hidden quark flavors, a symmetry-breaking scalar, and right-handed neutrinos, with a Z′ portal and allowed kinetic mixing. Separate two-light-flavor and one-light-flavor regimes produce different bound-state spectra and decay patterns; hidden flavor violation, Higgs mixing, and the heavy-quark glueball regime are additional scoped variations. A second construction uses heavy particles X charged under color and SU(nv), whose loops connect hidden glueballs to gluons through dimension-eight operators, with distinct prompt-adjoint, stable-adjoint, and stable-fundamental possibilities. Confinement scales, lifetime estimates, and collider benchmarks are conditional choices rather than defining properties of the whole family.','Complete for the assigned construction, representation, portal, and constituent-to-bound-state scope using all six supplied pages. Quantitative spectrum predictions and lifetime expressions affected by text extraction are qualified below.','2026-10-04T09:56:38+00:00','P43-20261004T094958Z-25be7d25','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(44,'0711.4866v1',1,'The paper presents secluded thermal dark matter whose annihilation into lighter metastable mediators can remain efficient while its interactions with the Standard Model become very small. Its elementary constructions include a Dirac WIMP coupled to a Higgsed U(1)′ vector with hypercharge kinetic mixing, a singlet fermion coupled to a Higgs-mixed singlet scalar, and two Z2-protected dark-matter choices coupled through a right-handed neutrino. Mediator decay before nucleosynthesis and establishment of thermal contact are separate requirements, with stronger mixing bounds arising if mediator decays themselves maintain equilibrium. A further construction uses hidden non-Abelian dynamics, heavy fermions charged under both sectors, and stable hidden baryons annihilating into lighter composites. The MeV-scale discussion distinguishes viable scalar-WIMP/vector and fermion-WIMP/scalar possibilities from branches constrained by excessive annihilation radiation or meson decays.','Complete for the assigned construction-focused scope using all supplied pages. Detailed experimental exclusion curves and historical comparison models are not reproduced; qualitative extensions without full Lagrangians are identified separately.','2026-10-04T10:03:43+00:00','P44-20261004T095640Z-b7c48045','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(45,'1411.3727v2',1,'The paper constructs confining Sp(Nc), SU(Nc) and O(Nc) hidden sectors with fundamental or vector fermions, whose chiral symmetry breaking produces dark pion multiplets. Their respective cosets are SU(2Nf)/Sp(2Nf), [SU(Nf)×SU(Nf)]/SU(Nf), and SU(Nf)/SO(Nf); the stated WZW topology conditions require Nf ≥ 2 for the first and Nf ≥ 3 for the others. A five-pion Wess-Zumino-Witten interaction provides 3 → 2 number changing, while mass and kinetic terms generate pion self-scattering. The minimal Sp(2) construction has two flavors, meaning four Weyl fermions, and five dark pions in a 5-plet of Sp(4). Relic-density estimates assume thermal contact with the Standard Model during freeze-out, and the paper separately considers flavor-breaking masses that leave one lightest pion as dark matter, provided the other pion flavors remain available through freeze-out.','Complete for the assigned construction and mechanism scope using all supplied pages. Portal dynamics and detailed group-factor polynomial transcription remain outside the established extraction.','2026-10-04T10:09:22+00:00','P45-20261004T100344Z-685d52aa','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(46,'1111.4482v3',1,'The paper constructs a hidden Abelian vector Higgs portal whose minimal mass-generating sectors possess a stabilizing Z2 parity, subject to the stated restrictions on charged matter and decay channels. It illustrates Stückelberg mass generation with either a directly Higgs-dependent mass or a hidden scalar mediator, and separately gives a charged-complex-scalar Higgs realization with charge-conjugation symmetry. These constructions forbid the linear vector portal couplings while permitting H̄H Xµ X^µ interactions. The vector-only description is an effective theory obtained through heavy-field elimination or higher-dimensional operators, with validity limited by longitudinal-vector scattering. Its phenomenological treatment assumes thermal vector dark matter, sets the vector quartic coupling to zero for simplicity, and studies invisible Higgs decay using the experimental constraints available at the time.','Complete for the assigned fields, mass mechanisms, parity, interactions, and effective-description assumptions. Numerical historical Higgs and dark-matter exclusion regions and the scalar-DM comparison are not comprehensively tabulated.','2026-10-04T10:13:55+00:00','P46-20261004T100942Z-395728f6','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(47,'1309.7223v2',1,'SARAH 4 provides declarations for gauge groups, global symmetries and either chiral superfields or separate scalar and fermion fields, with explicit conventions for representations and component charges. It builds gauge-fixed Lagrangians and exposes mass matrices, tadpole equations and vertices, while requiring care over invariant contractions and its restricted support for nonrenormalizable interactions. Its vacuum declarations support generation alignment and complex VEVs, and its SPheno and Vevacious interfaces connect these declarations to spectrum and vacuum calculations. The paper reports two-loop RGE support for general gauge theories and agreement with PyR@TE, alongside symmetry and anomaly checks. The appendix NMSSM and SM files are recorded as separate implementation examples, including unresolved inconsistencies in the printed charge conventions.','Complete for the assigned representation and validation scope using all supplied pages. Model inventory entries are not promoted to physical specifications; external algorithms and detailed validation results remain dependencies.','2026-10-04T10:22:29+00:00','P47-20261004T101356Z-4fab63e1','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(48,'1108.2040v2',1,'UFO represents an exported model specification as a standalone Python module containing particle, parameter, vertex, coupling, Lorentz, and coupling-order objects. Its conventions require mass-eigenstate particle declarations, distinguish independent real inputs from ordered algebraic dependencies, and express vertices in a possibly non-unique color ⊗ spin basis with explicit outgoing-particle and index conventions. The FeynRules interface exports all model vertices without filtering them for a particular generator, while the paper separately proposes a dimensional-regularization counterterm extension. A scoped SILH HWW example demonstrates nonstandard Lorentz structures, separation of SM and new-physics coupling orders, and the need to restrict amplitudes consistently with the O(ξ) Lagrangian truncation.','Complete for the assigned representation-focused seed scope using all supplied pages. This is a source-backed extraction of format requirements and examples, not independent validation of an implementation or a complete extraction of the SILH theory.','2026-10-04T10:27:21+00:00','P48-20261004T102230Z-6348a371','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(49,'1008.4884v3',1,'The paper classifies dimension-six operators built from SM fields, using an effective Lagrangian through Λ⁻² and identifying operators modulo equations of motion and total derivatives. With baryon number conserved, its basis has 59 operator types: 15 bosonic, 19 with two fermions and 25 with four fermions, before flavor assignments and Hermitian conjugates are counted. The dimension-six baryon-conserving basis also conserves lepton number, while the separate dimension-five operator Qνν violates lepton number; relaxing baryon conservation adds four four-fermion operator types. The treatment retains three generations, a single Higgs doublet and explicit gauge, flavor and Hermitian-conjugation conventions, and uses Fierz identities as well as EOM reductions to remove redundancies. These operators parameterize possible low-energy effects; their Wilson coefficients require a specified high-energy theory and do not identify a unique UV model.','Complete for the assigned classification scope. The inventory includes every dimension-six operator family, with explicit representatives from distinct sectors; it is not a component-by-component transcription for all flavor assignments.','2026-10-04T10:33:24+00:00','P49-20261004T102721Z-4756adbe','unchecked',NULL);
INSERT INTO "paper_summary_revisions" VALUES(50,'1711.10391v2',1,'The paper provides a classical matching dictionary from general heavy scalar, fermion and vector sectors to dimension-six SMEFT in the Warsaw basis, without assuming that the high-energy theory is renormalizable. Its assumptions include linearly realized Standard Model gauge symmetry, extra particles of spin at most one, and vector-like or Majorana extra fermions. Representative extracted entries connect a scalar singlet to Higgs and gluonic operators, a Majorana singlet to lepton–Higgs current operators, and scalar ζ and vector W exchange to four-fermion coefficients. The scalar-singlet example explicitly retains a dimension-five high-energy interaction, while the other displayed restrictions isolate selected contributions under stated assumptions. These are matching sectors and illustrative restrictions, not specifications of complete ultraviolet models.','Complete for the requested representative-example scope, not a transcription of the entire 48-representation dictionary. General matching assumptions and operator conventions accompany the selected relations; other representations and most mixed contributions are deferred.','2026-10-04T10:40:10+00:00','P50-20261004T103326Z-bbb49bd9','unchecked',NULL);
